# Python coding standard

This repository follows the Google Python Style Guide and enforces it with Ruff.

- 4-space indent; never tabs.
- 80-character line length (`[tool.ruff] line-length = 80`).
- `snake_case` functions and variables; `CamelCase` classes; `UPPER_SNAKE` module constants.
- Google docstrings with `Args:`, `Returns:`, `Raises:` (`[tool.ruff.lint.pydocstyle] convention = "google"`).
- Explicit imports only — never `from module import *`.
- Type-hint public APIs. Use built-in generics (`list[str]`, `dict[str, int]`).
- Prefer TypedDict / dataclass / NamedTuple / pydantic models over naked `dict[str, Any]` for structured data.
- Prefer `Sequence` / `Mapping` ABCs at boundaries when appropriate.
- Avoid `Any` on public APIs.
- Use modern builtins (`list[str]`, `dict[str, int]`, `|` unions).
- Do not add comments that restate the code. Do not reformat unrelated files.

The machine-readable standard is `pyproject.toml`. If a style choice is not in Ruff config, do not invent one — match neighboring files.

Before finishing Python work, follow `.cursor/rules/python-lint.mdc`.
